+# Agent Note: Logged execution-history selection
+
+Status: implemented
+
+English | [中文](2026-09-16-history-selection.zh.md)
+
+## Problem
+
+Alternative executions need one chronological audit without feeding abandoned alternatives to subsequent model requests. Replacing message text cannot also select request headers, context contributions and tool evidence.
+
+## Decision
+
+A required history/selected event references ordered raw-event ranges and a consecutive selection version. The [Session reader](../../../../packages/core/session/README.md#selected-execution-history) derives a selected read and folds message replacements within it. The raw log remains unchanged. The Loop uses that history for messages, prompt/header state and authored-prefix deduplication. Request reconstruction uses the raw prefix at that request, so later selections cannot change it.
+
+A wire projection carries selection outside paginated event windows. Conversation assembly filters records before Chat and Trajectory consume them. Lifecycle, inbox, resource use and model-selection intent remain chronological. Admission consumers may acknowledge their own already-persisted operations without another enqueue.
+
+Replay producers can register user-bubble presentation while retaining plugin provenance in the log. The user-action slot supplies raw message coordinates so consumers can edit an earlier input through their own history operation. These client registrations change neither roles nor model requests.
+
+Current validation checks original records and selected replacement relationships before producing a private same-coordinate input for the frozen released validator. Released format generations remain unchanged. Their validators retain request and tool lifecycle checks. The installed Session validates original surface placement and replacements; the adapter also checks system messages against their open steps. This extends [retained tool recovery](2026-09-13-retained-tool-outcomes.md) without rewriting historical receipts.
+
+## Alternatives considered
+
+Truncating JSONL would lose audit evidence. A second Session per alternative would move grouping and cross-session state into every consumer. Filtering only model messages would leave prompt and UI histories inconsistent. Changing frozen released validators would erase the meaning of their accepted formats.
+
+## Consequences
+
+Event and turn coordinates never rewind. Selection is explicit, requires earlier references and changes no tool outcome. Consumers own node grouping, operation deduplication and restoration of application state. Shared checks cover invalid references, raw-prefix reconstruction, admission, client selection and Session/SDK replay. The required event does not change structural Session format; unaware readers reject it.
